Day 5 of my 300-day cybersecurity journey, and I've completed the Windows Command Line room on TryHackMe.

I've spent most of my time in Linux terminals, but Windows dominates enterprise environments. If I can't navigate the Windows CLI efficiently, I can't respond to incidents, hunt threats, or automate defenses. Today, I changed that.

📋 What I Learned
File System Navigation

Used cd, dir, tree to explore directory structures without touching the mouse.

Created and removed directories with mkdir and rmdir.

Copied, moved, and deleted files using copy, move, del—bulk operations that save hours.

File Management Advanced

Used xcopy and robocopy for robust file replication with error recovery.

Viewed file content with type and more for quick inspection.

Process Management

Listed running processes with tasklist and filtered results using parameters.

Terminated unresponsive applications with taskkill /PID—essential for incident response.

Understood how attackers use process manipulation to evade detection.

Network Troubleshooting

Diagnosed IP configuration with ipconfig /all.

Tested connectivity and latency using ping -t for continuous monitoring.

Mapped active connections with netstat -anob to spot suspicious outbound traffic.

Resolved DNS issues using ipconfig /flushdns.

System Information

Retrieved OS details, hardware specs, and uptime with systeminfo.

Identified current user context with whoami.

Displayed hostname and network details using hostname.

Command Help & Navigation

Used command /? to explore help documentation for every tool.

Cleared the terminal with cls for a clean workspace.

⚠️ Problems I Faced & How I Solved Them
Problem	Solution
netstat -b required admin privileges	Ran Command Prompt as Administrator to view process names
Overwhelmed by netstat output	Used netstat -anb | findstr ESTABLISHED to filter active connections
Forgot syntax for advanced robocopy options	Used robocopy /? to explore mirror and logging options
Couldn't locate a specific file	Used dir /s filename to search recursively through directories
